{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And He brought him outside.</b> According to its plain sense, He brought him out from his tent to the outside to see the stars. According to the midrash, He said to him: Go forth from your astrology, for you saw in the constellations that you are not destined to beget a son — Abram has no son, but Abraham has a son; Sarai will not give birth, but Sarah will give birth — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 15:5). Now behold, Abram did beget Ishmael. But the meaning of the midrash is that Abram sought a son to inherit him, as he said, <b>and behold, the son of my house shall inherit me</b>, and the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him, \"This one shall not inherit you, but rather he who shall come forth from your innards — he shall inherit you,\" and go forth from your astrology: Abram does not beget a son to inherit him, but Abraham does beget a son to inherit him. And it is possible that the astrology concerned their pairing, that Abram and Sarai would not beget from one another, but Abraham and Sarah would beget. But in my opinion, Sarah is added in the midrash to say that the same applied also to Sarah, but the Holy One, blessed be He, did not now promise him regarding Sarah, and even at the time of the prophecy of circumcision Abraham still doubted whether Sarah would give birth:"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "Ramban argues that Rashi's midrash should be understood as referring specifically to the question of an heir rather than to childlessness in general, since Abram already had Ishmael."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:29:35.369725625Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}